WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.784
Look at a microwave's door.

00:00:01.794 --> 00:00:04.148
Those tiny holes trap the waves.

00:00:04.158 --> 00:00:06.915
Inside, a part called a magnetron makes

00:00:06.925 --> 00:00:08.269
the microwaves.

00:00:08.279 --> 00:00:11.178
They bounce around off the metal walls.

00:00:11.188 --> 00:00:12.978
When they hit food, they make the

00:00:12.988 --> 00:00:16.154
water molecules in it twist and vibrate.

00:00:16.164 --> 00:00:18.902
That movement turns the
microwaves' energy into

00:00:18.912 --> 00:00:21.011
heat, and the food cooks.

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.481
Each of these waves is about twelve

00:00:23.491 --> 00:00:24.954
centimetres long.

00:00:24.964 --> 00:00:26.851
The holes in the door's metal mesh

00:00:26.861 --> 00:00:29.551
are much smaller, so microwaves can't get

00:00:29.561 --> 00:00:31.504
through, but light can.

00:00:31.514 --> 00:00:33.871
And the turntable spins your food, so

00:00:33.881 --> 00:00:35.581
it cooks more evenly.
